<onlyinclude>'''Thin-film deposition''' grows or places a very thin solid coating on a surface, often only nanometres to micrometres thick. In chipmaking, such coatings can conduct electricity, insulate neighbouring parts, form semiconductor layers, control light, or protect delicate structures. The method and process conditions determine how evenly the coating covers a flat wafer or a deep trench.</onlyinclude>

[[Thin-film deposition/Technical|Read the technical explanation]]

== How it works ==
* '''Physical vapour deposition (PVD):''' atoms are released from a source by evaporation or sputtering, travel through a low-pressure chamber and settle on the wafer.
* '''Chemical vapour deposition (CVD):''' gaseous chemicals react to form a solid coating on or near the surface.
* '''Atomic layer deposition (ALD):''' alternating precursor exposures cause self-limiting surface reactions, allowing fine thickness control over many cycles.

== Why choose one method? ==
PVD is useful for many metallic coatings, but shadowing can make coverage uneven in narrow, deep features. CVD can cover complex shapes, although temperature and reaction chemistry matter. ALD can provide highly conformal thin coatings when reactants penetrate the features, but the repeated cycles can be slow.

Deposition ''adds'' material. Etching removes material; photolithography defines where subsequent processing acts.

== Limits ==
A coating with the right thickness may still fail if it contains impurities, cracks, weak adhesion, stress, or defects at its interface with the wafer. Film thickness and coverage must be measured, not assumed.

